```java
import java.util.*;
import java.lang.*;

public class Solution {
    public int modp(int g, int n, int p) {
        long res = 1;
        long base = g % p;
        long exponent = n;

        while (exponent > 0) {
            if (exponent % 2 == 1) {
                res = (res * base) % p;
            }
            base = (base * base) % p;
            exponent = exponent / 2;
        }
        return (int) res;
    }
}
```

The mathematical foundation of this solution relies on the properties of modular arithmetic, specifically that (a * b) % p = ((a % p) * (b % p)) % p. By breaking down the exponent n into its binary representation, we can compute the result by squaring the base at each step and multiplying the result by the current base if the corresponding bit in the binary representation of n is one.

